  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 320 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms\nare expected through this evening, primarily from the Piedmont \nthrough central VA and the Eastern Shore. Damaging wind gusts \nare possible with any thunderstorms, with the greatest threat \nfrom the north-side of the Richmond metro and through the \nNorthern Neck and southeast MD for this afternoon and evening.\n\nGOES water vapor channels depict SW flow aloft this afternoon \nbetween and upper trough over the Great Lakes and a subtropical \nridge offshore. There is a shortwave trough over the upper Ohio \nValley embedded within the flow and scattered convection has \ndeveloped well in advance of this feature along the higher \nterrain of the Appalachians and in vicinity of a lee side \ntrough. Meanwhile, surface high pressure is centered well \noffshore of the Southeast coast. Hot and humid with temperatures\nin the lower 90s and dewpoints in the lower to mid 70s. This \nresults in heat indices in the upper 90s to lower 100s.\n\nThe aforementioned shortwave trough is expected to push E \nacross the northern Mid-Atlantic through this afternoon and \nevening. Effective bulk shear is around 25-30kt, and should \nremain similar through early this evening. MLCAPE has reached \n2000-2500J/kg per SPC mesoanalysis, and may evening increase to \n2500-3000J/kg over the next 2-3 hours. DCAPE is about \n800-1000J/kg and is expected to remain steady through peak \nheating, with steep low-level lapse rates ~8C/km, and max 2-6km \nAGL lapse rates are even ~7C/km per the latest analysis. Given \nsufficient shear and instability, initial cellular convection \nhas the potential to organize into line segments, with the main \npoint of uncertainty through the afternoon and evening being how\nwidespread the convection becomes. The main threat will be \ndamaging wind gusts with 800-1000J/kg of DCAPE and steep low- \nlevel lapse rates, but some large hail is possible in any \ninitial cellular convection. SPC expanded the marginal risk area\nsouthward this morning to basically I-64 from the \nLouisa/Fluvanna line to the north- side of the RIC metro, then \neastward to about Wallops Island. A slight risk has been added \nimmediately N of the local area. Much of the area is in a \nmarginal ERO. However, showers/tstms should have enough motion \nto limit the excessive rainfall threat.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Heat and humidity will persist through the \nremainder of the weekend and into the middle of next week. A low\nchance of afternoon and evening showers and thunderstorms \ncontinues Sunday, mainly dry Monday, with diurnal chances of \nshowers/thunderstorms returning Tuesday through Thursday.\n\nThe subtropical ridge offshore will continue to prevail Sunday \nas the Great Lakes trough shifts to the Northeast. Deterministic\nmodels and ensembles continue to hint at a more zonal pattern \nacross the area by early next week, with the strongest flow \nremaining north of the area. Meanwhile, at the surface high \npressure continues to thrive off the coast continuing to usher \nin heat and moisture. Overall a rather typical stagnant late \nsummer pattern is expected with temperatures on the warm-side of\nseasonal averages. Dewpoints will struggle to mix out through \nthe day and remain in the low to locally mid 70s during peak \nheating. The mix of heat and humidity will allow heat indices to\nbe in the upper 90s and low 100s, but locally ~105F from the \nnorthern shore of the Albemarle Sound though southside Hampton \nRoads.\n\nA subtle boundary settles into the area later tonight and \nlinger Sunday. This along with the sea-breeze could trigger \nadditional showers/tstms Sunday. High-res models continue to \nstruggle with the extent and location of convection, but the \n12z/08 HREF suggests at least slight chc to low chc PoPs for \nmuch of the area. Locally heavy rain will be the primary threat.\nMainly dry Monday with generally deep layer westerly flow. \nPotentially more unsettled energetic WNW flow Tuesday through \nThursday (highest PoPs N) as a stronger trough dives SE toward \nNew England. This trough may eventually push a stronger cold \nfront through the region later in the week or early next \nweekend, but confidence in this remains low at this time.",
